TEXT -3480 -2368 Left 6 ;Step 1: Answer this Pop-Quiz Question\n \n1. What is this stage called?\n \n2. Calculate the DC operating point for Q1 (VB, VE, VC, IB, IC, IE). Assume BETA = 200\n \n3. What is the region of operation for Q1\n \n4. What is the voltage gain, V(OUT_STAGE_1)/V(IN_STAGE_1), if there is any?\n \n5. What are the benefits of RE_Q1, if any?\n \n6. Cblock_1, RB_TOP_Q1, and RB_BOT_Q1 form what kind of filter circuit? \nWhat's the corner frequency of that filter circuit?
TEXT 152 -2328 Left 6 ;Step 2: Answer this Pop-Quiz Question\n \n1. What is this stage called? Why did we use Q2 and Q3 as shown below?\n \n2. Calculate the DC operating point for Q2 and Q3 (VB, VC, VE, IC, IB, IE). Assume BETA = 200\n \n3. Is the small-signal input impedance (including bias resistors) of this stage - low, moderate, or high?\nWhat type of electrical signal is best connected to the input of this stage (voltage or current)?\n \n4. With the microphone input signal source set to 0V, and treating the load as disconnected, is the output impedance of this stage - low, moderate, or high?\nWhat type of DEPENDENT, CONTROLLED, source output is this best modeled as? Controlled current source or controlled voltage source?\n \n5. What is the total gain of these two cascaded stages together? Why didn't I connect my AC-coupled, loudspeaker load directly to the output of Stage 1?\nWhy did I have to do all this extra work of adding Stage 2?
TEXT -2616 944 Left 6 ;Step 3: Run an AC Simulation and verify your calculated operating point and your gain.\n \nStep 4: Run a transient simulation for 10 mVpp, 1kHz sine wave at the input, monitor the load voltage.\n \nStep 5 (Nice to Have): Measure frequency response with Bode 100 and 16 Ohm loudspeaker load, or 16 Ohm dummy resistor. Does the measured bode plot match the simulation?
